! mcp_bootstrap_p170.strap -- THE SIO AND RIO ROUTINES, both of them
! whole: KSIO and KRIO, printed page 170.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.177, header READ and not assumed:
! the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID JC000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  170".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK. <<<
! Rendered at 600 dpi across the right-hand half of the form: the
! word "LOCATION" is printed at the right-hand end of the heading
! row and NOTHING follows it but the dashed rule to the margin.
! That is what docs/transcription_workflow.md predicts -- the field
! is LINE 1'S location, and line 1 here is an asterisk banner that
! compiles nothing.  A blank is NOT a zero; no digits were read off
! the rule.  This page's only statement about its own origin is its
! own printed "SLC,$" on line 4.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-TWO PRINTED LINES, and they are TWO COMPLETE ROUTINES:
!
!   lines  1- 3   banner, "**** SUPPRESS I/O INTERRUPT ****"
!   line   4      "SLC,$" at 220410.40  (location-column asterisk)
!   lines  5-11   K SIO, seven instructions, ending "B,K SUPP"
!   lines 12-13   two bare comment cards (a "-" and nothing else)
!   lines 14-16   banner, "**** RELEASE I/O INTERRUPTS ****"
!   line  17      "SLC,$" at 220415.00  (location-column asterisk)
!   lines 18-32   K RIO, fifteen instructions, ending "B,KUNSTC"
!
! No DD, no SYN, no VF, no reservation, no forward SLC.  The page
! is CONTIGUOUS from 220410.40 to 220427.40 with no hole of any
! kind: 14 half words + 8 full words = 960 bits = 15 words, and
! 220427.40 - 220410.40 = 0o17 words = 15 words exactly.
!
! >>> C22-6728-1 DESCRIBES THIS PAGE IN TYPESET PROSE, LINE BY
!     LINE. <<<  "INTERRUPT MODE CONTROL ROUTINES / SIO and RIO
!     Routines":
!
!       "The SIO routine is entered at KSIO.  It identifies the
!        requestor by the status of the level bit (SL) and turns
!        on the SIO bit (SSIO) in the corresponding program status
!        table (SPROGS).  It adjusts the address in STIC, and
!        returns via the service op return routine (KSUPP)."
!
!     That is lines 5-11 in order: line 5 loads SL, lines 6-7
!     index SPROGS by it, line 8 is CM1111 -- turns the SSIO bit
!     ON -- lines 9-10 adjust STIC, line 11 is "B,K SUPP".
!
!       "The RIO routine is entered at KRIO (Figure 120).  It
!        turns off the SIO bit (SSIO) in the requestors program
!        status table (SPROGS), and if the requestor is auto-
!        stacked or has no interrupts stacked, returns via the
!        service-op return routine (KSUPP).  Otherwise, low
!        registers are saved in SLRPP or SLRMCP according to the
!        level of the requestor, and control is given to the
!        unstack routine (KUNSTC) to release the first stacked
!        interrupt."
!
!     That is lines 18-32 in order, including the detail that the
!     ONLY difference between the two routines' first six
!     instructions is CM1111 (line 8, ON) against CM0000 (line 21,
!     OFF, comment "-TURN OFF SIO BIT").  Line 24 is "auto-
!     stacked" (BB on S AS -> K SUPP); lines 25-26 are "has no
!     interrupts stacked" (LC,8,S Q K($9) then BXCZ -> K SUPP);
!     line 28 is "according to the level of the requestor" (BB on
!     S L); lines 29 and 31 are "saved in SLRPP or SLRMCP"; lines
!     30 and 32 are "control is given to the unstack routine
!     (KUNSTC)".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-TWO LINES, AND NOTHING FELL OFF EITHER END -- THREE WAYS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY SAID 20 ROWS, 220410.40..220427.0, KIND "mixed" with
! one trailing "+".  IT IS SHORT BY TWELVE AND ITS KIND IS WRONG.
! There is no SYN, no VF and no data card anywhere on this page;
! the "+" is survey noise.  Its first location is right (it is
! line 4) and its last, "220427.0", is line 32's 220427.00 with a
! digit dropped.  Three independent ways of knowing the real
! extent:
!
! (1) THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN, read at 600 dpi, glyph by glyph.
!     Lines 1-13 carry JC000001..JC000013 and lines 14-32 carry
!     JD000001..JD000019 -- two unbroken runs, no repeats, no
!     gaps, 13 + 19 = 32.  The header's own "CARD ID JC000001"
!     names line 1's card, so the TOP of the page is pinned by
!     IBM twice.  Below JD000019 is blank form.
!
!     >>> SO A CARD DECK CAN BE SHORTER THAN A PAGE. <<<  printed
!     p.168 established that a deck can be exactly one page (JA);
!     p.169's header opens JB.  THIS page carries the WHOLE of
!     deck JC (13 cards, the SIO routine and its banner) and then
!     starts JD on line 14.  The prefix tracks the ROUTINE, not
!     the page -- which is a sharper statement than p.168 could
!     make, and it predicts that JD CONTINUES PAST this page only
!     if the RIO routine does.  It does not: line 32 is RIO's last
!     instruction, so p.171 must open a NEW prefix.  >>> CHECKED:
!     PDF p.178's header reads "CARD ID KA000001". <<<  So JD is
!     exactly the 19 cards on my lines 14-32, and the prefix
!     tracks the ROUTINE.
!
! (2) THE LOCATION COLUMN CLOSES ON ITSELF.  Every one of the 22
!     emitted statements' printed locations follows from the one
!     above it by exactly the width of the instruction printed
!     there -- 14 half words, 8 full words, no gap.  A dropped
!     row would not balance (see the arithmetic above).
!
! (3) THE LINE-NUMBER COLUMN runs 1..32 with no break and line 32
!     is the last printed thing on the form.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SECOND "SLC,$" IS WRITTEN AS "SLC,$" AND THAT MAKES THE
! PAGE SELF-CHECKING
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 17 prints "SLC,$" at 220415.00.  An SLC emits nothing, so
! that printed location IS the counter arriving from line 11 --
! IBM's own assembler stating it.  THIS DECK WRITES "SLC,$" there,
! not a hard origin, so the value is CHECKED and not asserted:
!
!   * line 11 is "B,K SUPP" at 220414.40, a HALF WORD, so the
!     counter reaches 220415.00 if and only if lines 5-11 are all
!     the right width;
!   * line 18's !CHECK is at 220415.00, so a mis-sized instruction
!     anywhere in K SIO moves every K RIO row and fails loudly.
!
! Only ONE number on this page is asserted rather than checked:
! the origin 220410.40 on line 4, which this deck must write hard
! so the page assembles standalone.  A hard SLC cannot disagree
! with an arriving counter -- it overrides it silently (workflow,
! WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE item 2) -- so THAT join has to be
! compared BY HAND against printed p.169's exit.  The printed form
! is "SLC,$" and the printed location is 220410.40.
!
! Note the ".40": the offset inside an (8) literal is OCTAL and is
! written exactly as printed.  "(8)220410.0" would move all 22
! rows back 32 bits.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS, AND WHAT SETTLED EACH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIS PAGE DROPS INK ON ROUND GLYPHS HEAVILY, and it proves it in
! three different columns:
!
!   OCTAL FIELDS (a C cannot occur there): line 9 prints
!   "COCCOO.45", line 21's second word prints "001000.CC", line 10
!   prints "215265.C5", line 22 prints "COCCOO.45".
!
!   ENGLISH COMMENTS: lines 7 and 20 both print "-PROG STAT TABLE
!   FCR SL" for FOR, while line 5's "-DETERMINE LEVEL OF IC" and
!   line 21's "-TURN OFF SIO BIT" print closed O's a few
!   characters away.  The comment column is English and FCR is not
!   a word.  >>> This is the clearest same-page demonstration in
!   the block that THIS DRUM PRINTS O AS C, and it is why the two
!   label calls below are made the way they are. <<<
!
! Per the workflow that licenses nothing by itself; each call is
! settled on what PRODUCED the glyph or on an independent press.
!
! (1) >>> "K SIC" IS "K SIO" AND "K RIC" IS "K RIO". <<<  At 1800
!     dpi line 5's name reads S-I-C and line 18's reads R-I-C,
!     both with an open final glyph.  SETTLED OUTRIGHT BY THE
!     TYPESET MANUAL, a different press:
!
!       C22-6728-1, section "INTERRUPT MODE CONTROL ROUTINES",
!       sub-heading "SIO and RIO Routines" (docs/ocr/
!       C22-6728-1_7030_MCP_Sys_Analysis_Guide.txt lines
!       36990-37015; the OCR carries no page footer there, so the
!       citation is by HEADING and not by a page number I did not
!       read): "The SIO routine is entered at KSIO...  The RIO
!       routine is entered at KRIO (Figure 120)."
!
!       C22-6728-1's service-op entry table (same file, lines
!       4154-4161): "$SIO 32.0 KSIO" and "$RIO 32,32 KRIO".
!
!     COUNTED BY ME, per revision, not inherited:
!
!                       C22-6728   C22-6728-1
!         KSIO              2           2
!         KRIO              3           3
!         KSIC              0           0
!         KRIC              0           1
!
!     The single "KRIC" is at OCR line 37331 of the -1 revision,
!     inside a garbled ASCII rendering of a flowchart box that
!     also prints "KWALT" for KWAIT -- OCR noise off a FIGURE, not
!     prose, and the same O/C confusion read by a worse reader.
!
!     The semantics agree to the instruction: SIO turns the bit
!     ON (line 8, CM1111) and RIO turns it OFF (line 21, CM0000,
!     comment "-TURN OFF SIO BIT").
!
! (2) >>> AND A DIFFERENT PAGE OF THIS VERY LISTING PRINTS BOTH
!     ADDRESSES. <<<  Keep the two claims apart: the MANUAL gives
!     the SPELLING, and PDF p.185 (printed p.178, the operation
!     branch table) gives the ADDRESSES, off a page nobody here
!     transcribed:
!
!       line 34   220763.00   220410.50 00   B,K SIO  -STACK I/O INTERRUPTS
!       line 35   220763.40   220415.10 00   B,K RIO  -RELEASE I/O INTERRUPTS
!
!     A plain B prints target+.10, so those are 220410.40 and
!     220415.00 -- EXACTLY this page's two labels.  (That page's
!     volume OCR garbles the first name to "K STO", which is the
!     point: the ADDRESS is the witness there, not the spelling.
!     The volume OCR of a page is never a witness for that page,
!     but p.178 is a different page from p.170, so its printed
!     ADDRESSES are a legitimate second reading of where my
!     labels are.)
!
! (3) LINE 9'S BYTE IS "05", not "C5", AND THE PAGE PRINTS ITS OWN
!     CONTROL.  Line 9 and line 22 are THE SAME STATEMENT,
!     "V+I,2,.32", printed twice on one page in the same character
!     positions -- the strongest rung of the workflow's glyph
!     ladder.  At 1800 dpi line 9's byte opens ("C5") and line
!     22's is closed ("05").  Dropout opens a closed glyph and
!     never closes an open one, so the byte is 05.  Corroborated
!     by what produced it: line 7's V+I prints a clean "05" and
!     the operand has no index.
!
! (4) LINE 10 AND LINE 23 PRINT "215265.C5 30" FOR "215265.05 30".
!     The .C5 is an OCTAL field, so the C is impossible.  The same
!     word is printed by printed p.168 lines 7 and 34 for the same
!     statement "SV,2,S TIC", and the 0o05 is 2J+1 for J=2, not
!     S TIC's offset.
!
! (5) LINE 21'S SECOND WORD PRINTS "001000.CC FC" AND IS
!     "001000.00 F0".  Four impossible C's in the octal field; the
!     byte's low nibble is i2, the OFFSET INDEX, and
!     "CM0000(BU,1),S SIO($9)" has no second operand, so i2 is 0.
!     THE PAGE PRINTS THE CONTROL thirteen lines above: line 8 is
!     the matching CM1111 and prints "001000.36 F0" with a CLOSED
!     0.  printed p.18 prints that connective word six times
!     (workflow), and printed p.141 line 6 prints line 21's word
!     exactly, "000000.75 89 001000.00 F0", for the identical
!     statement "CM0000(BU,1),SSIO($9)".
!
! (6) "K MOVE" -- see WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED (A).  Recorded
!     here only as: at 1800 dpi the second glyph is open ("MCVE"),
!     and MCVE is not a word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK -- EXACTLY TWO, BOTH ON "SLC,$"
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   line  4   220410.40 *   SLC,$
!   line 17   220415.00 *   SLC,$
!
! Read at 900 dpi; no other row on the page carries the mark.
!
! RECORDED AND NOTHING PROPOSED, per the workflow.  What this page
! ADDS is only that it is consistent with NEITHER surviving rule:
! "every printed SLC,$ row carries it" was killed by printed p.151
! line 4, and "only SLC rows carry it" was killed by printed p.168
! lines 21 and 36 (a BB and a labelled VFL connective).  A page
! agreeing with a dead rule is not evidence for it.  No ink-column
! figure is quoted here: on this render the mark sits at the right
! edge of mcp_columns' column 2 (the LOCATION run, x 913-1357 at
! 300 dpi) and not in a run of its own, so the per-column ink
! number p.168 cited would not measure the same thing.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PRINTED SPACING, RECORDED (the statements below use the
! unspaced forms, as every landed deck does; a STRAP name ignores
! blanks)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   NAMES:      "K SIC" (line 5, = K SIO), "K RIC" (line 18,
!               = K RIO), "K MOVE" (line 31)
!   OPERANDS:   "S L", "S PROG S", "S SIO", "S TIC", "S AS",
!               "S Q K", "S TLR", "S LR PP", "S LR MCP",
!               "K SUPP", "K MOVE", "KUNSTC" (always unspaced)
!   AND NOTE LINE 31 HAS A SPACE AFTER THE COMMA that line 29
!   lacks: line 29 prints "T,15,S TLR,S LR PP" and line 31 prints
!   "T,15,S TLR, S LR MCP".  Both compile to the same shape; the
!   difference is in the punched card and is recorded because it
!   is printed.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS PAGE PINS, AND WHAT IT ONLY CORROBORATES
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! PINNED TO THE BIT (the printed word changes if the offset
! changes by one -- the symbol lands in a full 24-bit A24 field or
! in a VFL address):
!   S L     215001.04   lines 5, 18 and 28  (SCOMRG+0.04), THREE
!                       printings, two of them as a VFL address
!                       and one as a branch-on-bit operand
!   S SIO   000000.75   lines 8 and 21 (0o75 = 61 decimal)
!   S AS    000000.31   line 24 (0o31 = 25 decimal)
!   S Q K   000000.40   line 25.  The printed .60 is the symbol's
!                       offset PLUS 2J for J=8 (= 0o20), so
!                       0o60 - 0o20 = 0o40 = 32 decimal, which is
!                       printed p.109 line 9's defining card
!                       exactly.  This page pins it to the bit.
!
! HALF WORD ONLY -- the .00 or .40 is a reading of bit 32 and the
! five bits below it are UNOBSERVABLE (workflow, WHAT THE ORACLE
! CANNOT SEE item 1):
!   S TIC    215265.00  lines 10 and 23 (.05 is 2J+1 for J=2)
!   S PROG S 215021.00  lines 7 and 20 (.23 is 2J+1 for J=9)
!   K SUPP   216242.00  lines 11, 24 and 26
!   S TLR    215230.00  lines 29 and 31
!   S LR PP  215266.00  line 29
!   S LR MCP 215362.00  line 31
!   KUNSTC   222037.00  lines 30 and 32
!   $L       000010.00  lines 6 and 19 (.22 is 2J for J=9); $L is
!                       the left half of the accumulator, word 8,
!                       C22-6531 Appendix A
!
! THIS PAGE'S OWN LABELS, checked by check_rows against the
! LOCATION column:
!   K SIO   220410.40   line  5
!   K RIO   220415.00   line 18
!   K MOVE  220426.00   line 31 (and referenced by line 28, whose
!                       printed target "220426.34" checks it to
!                       the half word AND is produced from inside
!                       this deck)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT COULD NOT BE SETTLED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (A) >>> "K MOVE" IS UNFALSIFIABLE FROM THIS PAGE AND IS THE
!     PAGE'S ONE GENUINELY OPEN READING. <<<  It is referenced
!     exactly once, by line 28 on its own page, so no merge can
!     fail on it and no !CHECK can see it -- the workflow's
!     "a symbol that nothing else references rests on ink alone".
!     The evidence for MOVE: at 1800 dpi the second glyph is open,
!     this page demonstrably opens O's in its own comment column
!     ("FCR" for FOR, twice), and "MCVE" is not an English word
!     where every other label on these pages is an abbreviation of
!     one.  The manual's sentence that covers lines 28-31 -- "low
!     registers are saved in SLRPP or SLRMCP according to the
!     level of the requestor" -- is semantically exact but NAMES
!     NO SYMBOL, so it is not a witness to the spelling.  Counted:
!     "KMOVE"/"K MOVE" returns ZERO hits in either revision of
!     C22-6728 and zero hits in the volume OCR outside this page's
!     own line 28.  WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: a page branching to
!     220426.00 from outside this page.  None is known.  IF ONE
!     TURNS UP AND SPELLS IT DIFFERENTLY, THIS DECK IS WRONG.
!
! (B) THE THREE LABELS ARE INVISIBLE TO check_symbol_spellings.py,
!     and this must not be read as a pass.  That tool compares a
!     name against OTHER decks' readings of the same location;
!     nothing in the landed corpus references 220410.40, 220415.00
!     or 220426.00, because the page that branches to the first
!     two (printed p.178) has not landed.  So the tool is SILENT
!     on all three, not agreeing.  K SIO and K RIO rest on the
!     typeset manual (independent press, decisive); K MOVE rests
!     on ink plus English (item A).  >>> WHEN printed p.178 LANDS,
!     ITS TRANSCRIBER'S READING OF "B,K SIO" AND "B,K RIO" BECOMES
!     THE FIRST REAL TEST OF THIS DECK'S TWO ENTRY-POINT NAMES.
!     <<<
!
! (C) WHY LINES 12 AND 13 EXIST.  Two cards (JC000012, JC000013)
!     printing a bare "-" in the comment column and nothing else,
!     between K SIO's last instruction and the RELEASE banner.
!     They compile nothing.  Lines 1-3 and 14-16's banners carry
!     the same leading "-", so the "-" is the comment-card marker
!     and these two are EMPTY comment cards.  Why the deck carries
!     two blank cards here and p.168 carried none is not something
!     this page can say.
!
! (D) THE PREFIX LETTERS ARE STILL UNDECODED, although this page
!     narrows them.  p.168 showed a prefix can be exactly one page
!     (JA, the WAIT routine).  THIS page shows a prefix can cover
!     LESS than a page: JC is 13 cards, and JD starts on line 14 of
!     the same page.  Five consecutive prefixes are now known and
!     every one is exactly one ROUTINE:
!
!       JA  p.168           WAIT
!       JB  p.169           CHEX   (header read by p.168's agent)
!       JC  p.170 lines 1-13   SIO
!       JD  p.170 lines 14-32  RIO
!       KA  p.171           STRGE  (header read here, bounded)
!
!     "One prefix per routine" now has five data points and no
!     counterexample, but NOTHING IN docs/ DECODES THE LETTERS and
!     the second letter's reset (JD -> KA, not JE) is unexplained.
!     WHAT WOULD KILL IT: any page whose serials change prefix
!     mid-routine, or a routine spanning two prefixes.  NOT
!     PROPOSED AS A RULE -- recorded as a five-point pattern with
!     its own falsifier named.
!
! (E) THE ASTERISK BANNERS (lines 1-3, 14-16) ARE NOT TRANSCRIBED
!     CHARACTER BY CHARACTER.  They emit no bits and counting ~65
!     asterisks off a worn drum is a reading nobody can check.
!     Line 2's text is "SUPPRESS I/O INTERRUPT" and line 15's is
!     "RELEASE I/O INTERRUPTS", both centred; the serials are
!     counted.  (Note line 15's banner is the only one whose
!     asterisk run is BRACKETED by "-" at both ends.)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER LIMITATIONS HIT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! NONE.  Every statement below is the PRINTED form, handed to
! strap_asm first, and ALL TWENTY-TWO were accepted first time and
